# What install.sh prints at its end, for a package that writes no home.

post_install() {
	cat <<'EOF'
==> The examples of aish are in /usr/share/aish/examples and do nothing there.
    To use one, copy it to the directory of its kind under ~/.config/aish;
    each file tells at its top what it does:
      /usr/share/aish/examples/policy/*.cedar   ->  ~/.config/aish/policy/
      /usr/share/aish/examples/hooks/EVENT/*    ->  ~/.config/aish/hooks/EVENT/
      /usr/share/aish/examples/tools/*          ->  ~/.config/aish/tools/
    e.g.
      mkdir -p ~/.config/aish/tools && cp /usr/share/aish/examples/tools/weather ~/.config/aish/tools/
    Hooks and tools work from the next request, a policy from the next aish
    or, in a running one, after aish apply-config.
==> Start aish with a key of the model's API in the environment
    (AISH_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or OPENAI_API_KEY):
      aish
==> To run aish in place of your shell, either make aish the command of your
    terminal emulator (in its profile settings), or add this line at the end
    of ~/.bashrc (~/.zshrc for zsh):
      [[ -z ${AISH_SOCK-} && ${AISH_FALLBACK-} != $$ && $- == *i* ]] && command -v aish >/dev/null && exec aish
    The shell aish starts reads that file too and skips the line: AISH_SOCK is
    set there. $- == *i* keeps aish out of scripts; command -v keeps exec from
    closing the terminal once aish is removed. aish starts bash; for zsh,
    see https://github.com/GoldenDeals/aish#zsh. Should aish fail to start
    (a broken config, say), it prints why and starts your shell in its place,
    rc files and all, with its pid in AISH_FALLBACK, for the line to skip it:
    fix what is wrong there and run aish. Do not make aish your login shell
    (chsh): ssh with a command and scp run that with -c, which aish does not
    take.
EOF
}
